#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/id #uuid "b58fbe0d-bab7-57b3-931f-ef8d4e1990c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5634" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9},$ find $P(B).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "711b6630-ea78-53f6-b830-6ecaac9a468a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5636" :question/problem "Two cards are drawn from a standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c1a5898916d3156aa9acb1884f2ab9fd149c10ca2149574a50fc6147586691e6.png)-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set aside, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?\n\n*Hint: A standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/5f378bdc2525ccdfd98ae36dc698e3bdf3f328a76d6841faf2198ec37b34db9a.png)-card deck has ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d44ac804f533a60a0d252fbcddaec9693d8b989dc429bf03f3ef1f0587cf3453.png) suits with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/f3ae95c18ff411808c146e5d3bfc30f49380b8272511f1a90a3400209b2df340.png) cards in each suit.There is one ace in each suit.\n Hearts is one of the suits.*" :question/worked-solution "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/15fa693fff5bc0eb2a8851089612b5a3fb969c3fd5fbc7c0821e58e52ff4260d.png) be the event that the third card is an ace, and let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/86056525c7e84fd98e233f12873f4b7bd6ed728224c7c1afe986743f6cda0aa0.png) be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a143776333b936a72708b71c7deadb626e347a6c094630c9dbce894d550ed5bb.png)\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d18d46bb8861930c4c3e602e4eef58228e745671a5445823a11d088e0a9e43a8.png) are mutually exclusive events, i.e., ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/37f1cba75404eaf490f8ae5f3de37c1da606e372b6b2e1f1c9c2c23a9ee0f82d.png)\n\nWe can find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3abd50b1d24b25f8908834ec0b3ef94c630f1c8c5352c0528a381b4bddc30ed1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a73f6c30e5a00859bc7f04c5f08a59a978dcc714634187dd178a9fa2059d1578.png) by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards in the deck. Among the remaining ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/97314fc826b205001aa477df5cccb4ceb42aff037d8ccf663a40341a70629d9f.png) hearts and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/6da8ea22dec2b00f0f9fa815118a83e3877bfdba5149ea487f242a1839878b0e.png) aces. Therefore:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/ecf686508e7fe0e4454085c9af1c06ce613ebf9dc1ef4c5bc2f0f93a146a8a23.png)\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/456f4dde4ad17f8738a277bb3a6eb139db35bb20aaa85dba794f2f1730bd1495.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/id #uuid "a968e5dc-f9fc-5bf7-8879-5276e0512483" :question/math-academy-id "e-5727" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{7}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3-7}{12} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173d63d0-edfb-567b-b629-c32983d51142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312449} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd80d9d-fca1-528e-ba1c-ce0ebadb10bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312450} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4a75f-e2b5-58f9-84fe-0fb3d1b6eca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312451} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbd41b3-74c6-5290-bad3-f2db34023479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312452} {:answer/id #uuid "26c7e901-2298-59dc-b67b-68e0a0b592e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd80d9d-fca1-528e-ba1c-ce0ebadb10bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312450} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3b0e32-52dd-575d-b4ac-e22d9ee651f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38965b82-21b5-5bb0-b47b-24c8c7b13476" :question/math-academy-id "q-120513" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{3}{8},$ $P(B)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{17}{24},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{17}{24} & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{17}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+8-17}{24} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4933e66-9c41-5512-9652-45ef2de0ba94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312456} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f4f2fa-5d63-56a5-803c-4ef9e31d6943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312457} {:answer/id #uuid "aed63c87-db80-5143-8c53-6d84b4dcbef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312458} {:answer/id #uuid "815758e3-7fc8-55e5-baee-9e48553fe7ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312459} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5f7d66-f3e5-5df4-9f95-0ebe83016b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f4f2fa-5d63-56a5-803c-4ef9e31d6943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebd7b59b-37dd-57cc-90c6-2c37218a41da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c835ba5-746a-5d4a-a539-db7fadba80c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120524" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{8}{21}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{8}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{63-32}{84} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{84}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99a0c687-95d8-5749-bf9e-2f9b63990362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312463} {:answer/id #uuid "3737965e-5af6-5c5d-b9a1-50982b7e5dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312464} {:answer/id #uuid "35c2cf11-17d7-5c10-87ae-5e0a09d47cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312465} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbef13e-6dd1-5c06-b65f-4371e3c5491a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312466} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8fc34b-21b2-5295-be25-cccf6cde47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a0c687-95d8-5749-bf9e-2f9b63990362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312463} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c06ba9-bee7-5cd5-abe0-ed4fe50771c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1db7a6ec-d7f9-50c5-b3f9-3a121c39ded3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120526" :question/problem "A number is picked from $10$ to $19.$ What is the probability of picking an even number or a prime number?" :question/worked-solution "Let $E$ be the event that the picked number is an even number, and let $R$ be the event that the picked number is a prime number. Then the probability that the selected number is an even number or a prime number is $P(E\\cup R).$\n\nGiven that all prime numbers between $10$ and $19$ are odd, $E$ and $R$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(E\\cap R)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(E)$ and $P(R)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $10$ numbers from  $10$ to $19$, of which  $5$ are even, and $4$ are prime.   Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E) & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ P(R) & =\\frac{4}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E\\cup R) & =P(E)+P(R)-P(E\\cap R) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{4}{10}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb7efbc7-86e4-51d5-a953-2439a4a18d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312470} {:answer/id #uuid "69e0b292-fe54-54c9-bc4a-4aed2502b682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312471} {:answer/id #uuid "12bfca1f-8635-5a87-82b6-b210263fcc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312472} {:answer/id #uuid "723c0850-fa31-5c6c-b242-e21ed2e6de9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312473} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf521c5-9549-5401-bf47-24f4b8a631f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12bfca1f-8635-5a87-82b6-b210263fcc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312472} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf423359-8d32-5275-ba24-80d4806e070a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9f5f88f-94fe-59a3-858c-68b6a4ea8014" :question/math-academy-id "q-120545" :question/problem "On a shelf, there are $11$ novels and $9$ science books. $1$ of the novels and $3$ of the science books are illustrated. The illustrated novel is taken from the shelf and set apart. If a single book is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a novel or an illustrated book?" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ be the event that the selected book is an illustrated book, and let $N$ be the event that it is a novel. Then the probability that the selected book is a novel or an illustrated book is given by $P(I\\cup N).$\n\nSince the only illustrated novel was removed, there are no novels left on the shelf that are also illustrated. Therefore, $I$ and $N$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(I\\cap N)=0.$\n\nSince the illustrated novel was set apart, there are $10+9=19$ books remaining, of which $10$ are novels and $3$ are illustrated. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I) & =\\frac{3}{19}, \\\\ P(N) & =\\frac{10}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I\\cup N) & =P(I)+P(N)-P(I\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{19}+\\frac{10}{19}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+10}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d97ad7d-a83a-5e49-99eb-f79949cb7f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312477} {:answer/id #uuid "b6936ea4-ebd6-55d8-be7d-4bf96e5680ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312478} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ea7738-6e91-5073-804b-d7bcb8437d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312479} {:answer/id #uuid "e01531d9-b7a4-5078-b24a-aa6ab4cc8d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312480} {:answer/id #uuid "a61445f1-8a68-5adb-9914-f0a89b37c06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e01531d9-b7a4-5078-b24a-aa6ab4cc8d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312480} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b8c64ef-ccfe-5956-ab1b-e59eff2a7735" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5829032-59a0-5111-b1d8-47c7a5a05911" :question/math-academy-id "q-12549" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=0.25$, $P(B)=0.75$, $P(A\\cup B)=0.8$, determine whether  $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.8 & =0.25+0.75-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =0.25+0.75-0.8 \\\\  & =0.2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a039349e-409c-53c9-807e-87652659e7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094257} {:answer/id #uuid "b374a0bd-bad0-5861-b51a-9b4c63b48597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094258} {:answer/id #uuid "0d21f686-73b9-52bb-b0dd-621e0e31ef77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094259} {:answer/id #uuid "24ba5170-a5b7-5d3c-997a-9d8e3e378db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094260} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc3ea7-bfde-55fb-8f14-7f050b9b991a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a039349e-409c-53c9-807e-87652659e7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094257} :answer-field/id #uuid "13c1a5de-4031-5d1c-bdf4-13af166d4e70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b8787c0-54a9-5103-b996-02111db7628d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12551" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\frac{2}{7}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P(B).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-8}{28} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{28}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e0873c3-1a10-543f-85ab-8eebe87fcc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312484} {:answer/id #uuid "91893fc4-fd91-56ce-b44c-cede7f6c8404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312485} {:answer/id #uuid "976b1181-7a48-50a6-8bd4-8b8d94503cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312486} {:answer/id #uuid "125ca9ed-4c79-5d83-ba81-bae105b45e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312487} {:answer/id #uuid "3a63241c-df4f-51fc-8b18-2296350882ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e0873c3-1a10-543f-85ab-8eebe87fcc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312484} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3933ad-5091-5d8f-a7ac-5c0f282452ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b1aa864-facc-5f33-ab7f-7efe7ce4a1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-12552" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=0.5$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.7,$ find $P(B).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =0.7-0.5 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f094bd-4db2-58ba-92bd-7856fecd955e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312491} {:answer/id #uuid "61018851-76ff-537b-84ec-0a0082791012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312492} {:answer/id #uuid "9e04b427-0c9d-5679-b48c-6e1ab9bbeb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312493} {:answer/id #uuid "e672e55c-583b-594d-8997-3e2d055f54b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312494} {:answer/id #uuid "78f4c3bd-a10f-5ebd-b313-2b0c153883c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e04b427-0c9d-5679-b48c-6e1ab9bbeb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312493} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be57220-224e-59b9-9572-6140d3506fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ffaa74bf-70c5-5164-b2b4-295879e59db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-12554" :question/problem "A card is drawn from a standard $52$-card deck. The drawn card is the Ace of Spades and it is set aside. A second card is drawn from the deck. What is the probability that the drawn card is an ace or a spade?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits.*" :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The second card drawn is an ace,\" and $S$ the event \"The second card drawn is a spade.\" The required probability is given by  $P(A\\cup S).$ \n\nGiven that the Ace of Spades is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $S$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap S)=0.$\n\nSince the Ace of Spades was set aside, there are $51$ cards remaining,  of which $3$ are aces and $12$ are spades. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{3}{51}, \\\\ P(S) & =\\frac{12}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup S) & =P(A)+P(S)-P(A\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{51}+\\frac{12}{51}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+12}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7ff837-3676-594f-b677-4f59b6a79b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312498} {:answer/id #uuid "898e332d-7c13-5eb8-b9f4-56b1be2a5743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312499} {:answer/id #uuid "16966b06-e665-5163-b6fa-eec16728002a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312500} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba1b3fb-88dc-5145-9d8b-71fa80b76156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312501} {:answer/id #uuid "2d015b2e-df40-5d53-bcc5-015b0f87bfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16966b06-e665-5163-b6fa-eec16728002a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312500} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8fd6cc0-5195-5938-bda3-53708a6bd0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1ed0374-b4e0-5a94-b18c-7a2811425755" :question/math-academy-id "q-5616" :question/problem "A game show uses a roulette wheel divided into $50$ panels. There are $15$ blue, $15$ green, $15$ yellow, $2$ black, and $3$ white panels randomly shuffled on the roulette wheel. What is the probability that the roulette wheel stops on a white or green panel?" :question/worked-solution "Let $W$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a white panel, and let $G$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a green panel. Then the probability that the roulette stops on a white or green panel given by $P(W\\cup G).$\n\nGiven that the roulette will stop at only one panel, the events $W$ and $G$  are mutually exclusive, i.e., $P(W\\cap G)=0.$ \n\nWe can find $P(W)$ and $P(G)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $50$ panels in the roulette. Of those panels, $3$ are white, and $15$ are green. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =\\frac{3}{50}. \\\\ P(G) & =\\frac{15}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\cup G) & =P(W)+P(G)-P(W\\cap G) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{15}{50}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d7ee11-f78e-572b-83c4-5dbbe52dc1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095804} {:answer/id #uuid "2178839c-8153-53b8-a6fd-c73ee460f34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095805} {:answer/id #uuid "35f50752-0af2-55b8-b9f8-746e947acd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095806} {:answer/id #uuid "6debf44b-a834-58cd-a2ca-73a7e7b522b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095807} {:answer/id #uuid "70e06785-16b7-50d5-bc3e-9ed2f7d82c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97d7ee11-f78e-572b-83c4-5dbbe52dc1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095804} :answer-field/id #uuid "eacace29-2482-542f-894d-457f037aa9a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14e2d387-14dd-5f24-a5a0-2989b5d7369b" :question/math-academy-id "q-5617" :question/problem "Kevin is choosing a random name for his new character in a video game. If all names may only start with an uppercase letter, what is the probability that the generated name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\"?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the name starts with an \"R,\" and let $E$ be the event that the name starts with an \"E.\" Then the probability that the name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\" is given by $P(R\\cup E).$\n\nGiven that only one letter can be picked as the first letter, $R$ and $E$ are mutually exclusive events. i.e. $P(R\\cap E)=0.$\n\nThere are $26$ letters in the alphabet, and each letter has an equal chance of being randomly picked ($1$ out of $26$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =\\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ P(E) & =\\frac{1}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}+\\frac{1}{26}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a90ed4-0fe9-5f4e-902e-486d6cbdaef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312505} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2bd995-f03b-5cfc-9913-d8b0d43f2d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312506} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb21520-b106-58ef-a295-33a29b4f7bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312507} {:answer/id #uuid "71b7d574-892f-5726-9627-204ff6343d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312508} {:answer/id #uuid "3805a536-99f3-576c-871c-172d2e4c36e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b7d574-892f-5726-9627-204ff6343d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312508} :answer-field/id #uuid "1df0207b-a3f6-5530-9941-6b0657ede1cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89bb3e91-78b6-5987-8b83-97952b870bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-87286" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{2}{7},$ $P(B)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{10}{21},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{10}{21} & =\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{1}{3}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{10}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+7-10}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2ed765-b60f-50c5-96a5-3ea3312151aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312512} {:answer/id #uuid "6a25db9b-575b-55ad-9bc9-86380e98af08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312513} {:answer/id #uuid "a56d4ff4-e5af-5f04-95ad-5bcdc7051fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312514} {:answer/id #uuid "6f57882e-e4bb-5a32-b814-9771c13e8739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312515} {:answer/id #uuid "0eea9adb-50ed-522d-85b4-e4e5314e3b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f57882e-e4bb-5a32-b814-9771c13e8739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312515} :answer-field/id #uuid "68c100ea-75ba-5dc6-aa79-efb5c53642b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94a980d8-2b2e-5cf9-9a95-55c26c4f6102" :question/math-academy-id "q-87289" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{1}{4},$ $P(B)=\\frac{3}{10},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{19}{40},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{19}{40} & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12-19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acef46bb-4fcc-5173-b053-dbf262bc7150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312519} {:answer/id #uuid "66bfd159-86db-53fa-abe0-7117923b07bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312520} {:answer/id #uuid "da3ddabc-330a-5bcd-83c3-e2a1f6ddc275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312521} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d33adc-889a-5bd2-9c74-86b27c300d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312522} {:answer/id #uuid "47f7b184-a524-5423-96bd-17a9179937c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66bfd159-86db-53fa-abe0-7117923b07bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312520} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c28b8fc-03eb-5146-80b7-74cc91f223dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c41467e9-5d33-5f1d-b8e1-f115414e579c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87294" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{1}{3}$, $P(B)=\\frac{3}{8}$, $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{17}{24}$, determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{17}{24} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{17}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+9-17}{24}=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40416d54-d918-519c-b311-7cc1b58ac218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312526} {:answer/id #uuid "e81afcb0-2ed1-52b1-a408-f2146078e02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312527} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bddc01-065a-59b1-8781-61bc9a28b831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312528} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7f634b-ebe9-52a5-9843-ea09527a4730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7b44ab-fdc4-5edf-8f37-a8e2045f2f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7b44ab-fdc4-5edf-8f37-a8e2045f2f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312530} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e07e98-6c47-53f4-80f4-cfa30089cf76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e20e394-569b-5fe6-8d7e-276b93ef613e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87305" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{1}{15}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f826c59-0b4e-5ed6-b029-4ef26a46532f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312533} {:answer/id #uuid "6333b560-a0e3-57c1-86be-ca4d06fe4901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312534} {:answer/id #uuid "e60e6569-dba3-53fc-9050-11b9c25ada2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312535} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbb94e4-cb66-5f2c-920a-962789e93724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312536} {:answer/id #uuid "975d7a3d-6486-530a-9868-370d036170c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bbb94e4-cb66-5f2c-920a-962789e93724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced2a49d-ef9a-5ea3-aedd-a5bb6a1b22b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "610824ef-12b9-5ca4-a472-9a8e2ccc0745" :question/math-academy-id "q-87308" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{18}{25}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{4}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
